cards.description:
Parts of the model rest on assumptions because the supplier documents are missing:
- the ESC's CAN protocol, brake feedback and protections (R-14);
- the BMS frame layout (R-15);
- which controller board the robot really has (R-59).

The owner asks XMachines for the ESC manual and the BMS documentation; the lead can draft the request for the owner to send. If the data cannot be had, the fallback is to measure them on the bench by capturing the CAN traffic.

The robot works near people, on slopes and in greenhouse aisles, and no hazard analysis exists yet: the specs cite ISO 18497, but there is no hazard log. The functional safety engineer:
- writes the safety plan: the safety life cycle tailored to the project, the independence of the safety assessment, and the safety deliverables at each gate;
- identifies the hazards of every operating mode and abnormal case of the charter (ISO 12100, with the hazard list of ISO 18497), in the Safety Analysis Manager;
- rates each hazard, and sets its safety goal and the performance level it needs (the risk graph of ISO 25119 or ISO 13849-1);
- names the safety function that meets each goal.

The hazard log feeds the requirements baseline (G1-01) and the SRR (G1-05).

The project has no version control: every change is tracked by copying files to a snapshot folder by hand. The configuration and documentation engineer:
- puts the project folder under git, with Git LFS for the models, data and documents (.slx, .mat, .docx, .pdf) and a .gitignore for the generated, cache and results folders;
- sets up the MATLAB project's source control and the Simulink model compare;
- tags the G0 baseline, the state the owner approved on 2026-11-16, and adds a tag at each gate;
- changes the work rules: a commit per step in place of the manual snapshots, and a separate working copy for each engineer, so two engineers never edit the same files.

A remote copy, for example a private repository, is the owner's choice.

The G0 gate review (NASL-SHD-GRR-001) recorded lessons for the handbook, and CR-002 adds a role and deliverables that belong in the company's framework, not only in this project. The lead systems engineer issues NASL-EMS-HBK-001 v1.2 with:
- the G0 lessons: the charter before the first readiness check, and the counts taken from the registers;
- the functional safety engineer in the roles and the RACI, and the safety deliverables at each gate: the hazard log at G1, the safety concept at G2, the FMEA at G3, fault injection at G4, the safety case at G5;
- the model credibility deliverables at G2: the estimated-parameter register and the validation plan;
- version control in place of the manual snapshots;
- the schedule fields of a card, and the critical path.

It also adds the document type PRO (procedure) to the handbook's table of documents, for the first procedure, NASL-EMS-PRO-001 (making an engineering report, 2026-11-16).

And it brings chapter 11, the worked example of the descent card, to its end: G2-DSC closed on 2026-11-16 with NASL-SHD-ER-005.

The cards have predecessors but no dates. The lead systems engineer:
- adds planned and actual start and finish dates to the card fields, and a target date to each gate;
- writes a tool that computes the critical path from the predecessors and the card sizes, with durations calibrated on the cards done so far;
- shows the planned dates against the actual ones on the dashboard's Project tab;
- re-projects the dates at every gate review.

The owner's decision cards are milestones on the path, with their own target dates.

The communication links are modelled as better than they behave:
- the RC radio cannot produce a link-loss stop (R-12);
- the parity cases of S6c cannot fail (R-47);
- the companion's CAN2 input drops frames silently (R-48);
- the link emulations miss NTRIP reconnects, bus-off and BMS frame drops (R-81).

The comms engineer models these failure modes and the robot's reaction to each, and tidies the row test (R-85). The card ends with the comms PDR.

The drive model still uses the proposed 1 kW motor, while the robot has 750 W motors (R-13); the ESC, its CAN protocol and its protections are assumed (R-14). The drive engineer:
- moves the model to the as-built motor;
- models the ESC from its manual, and tests its protections and recovery paths (R-07);
- makes stall and brake faults detectable (R-42, STATUS 27);
- repeats the motor thermal test at the right rating (R-57).

The card ends with the drive PDR.

Guidance must keep the whole robot, implement included, clear of obstacles. Today the mower deck is invisible to the collision checks and to the clearance monitor (R-06), so S4 with the mower stops at its first lane-end wall. The GNC engineer:
- adds the deck footprint to every check;
- supervises docking (R-10);
- recovers a replan answer lost during a hold (R-11);
- settles the bypass method (R-24, decision 11) and the resume pivot (decision 12);
- fixes the bypass speed and the path analyser (R-41, R-49, R-50).

The card ends with the GNC PDR. It is the next card the owner named after the descent.

The system model's checks must be trustworthy. The integration engineer:
- runs the S3 endurance scenario, which has never run (R-20);
- makes the hold reasons name the real cause (R-40);
- unblocks the reference solver (R-62);
- makes the scenario criteria consistent (R-63);
- removes dead and duplicated parameters and silent stub builds (R-70 to R-72);
- replaces the hand-kept layout allowance (R-73);
- adds a deck-lowered report from the MCU to the interfaces.

The card ends with the integration PDR.

The firmware model targets an STM32H743 board, which the robot does not have (R-59). Once the owner names the real board, the MCU engineer re-targets the model, makes the task-overrun fault work on it (R-08), and implements the reset policy the owner chooses (R-36). The engineer also closes the safety gaps the review found:
- an arm edge that can come from the receiver (R-35);
- the restart on a slope (R-37);
- the input word at start-up (R-38);
- a lost feedback frame (R-39).

The card ends with the MCU and electronics PDR.

Navigation and perception must have known limits on the real robot. The sensors engineer:
- covers the zone in front of the body as the owner decides (decision 2);
- adds level calibration (R-21);
- puts both latency compensations on one clock (R-22);
- models GNSS integrity (R-43);
- allows re-alignment without RTK (R-44);
- makes a frozen IMU or LiDAR visible (STATUS 26, 28).

The engineer also checks the greenhouse localisation (R-23) and the scan matcher's limits (R-51, R-53). The card ends with the navigation PDR.

The power design must work with the real sealed pack and the real BMS. The review found four gaps:
- the hot-weather charge depends on a cooling the sealed pack does not have (R-04);
- the precharge cannot complete with the ESC's standby load (R-09);
- the BMS model is not the robot's (R-15);
- the shift energy budget disagrees with the row tests (R-19).

The power engineer settles these with the owner's decisions on cooling, precharge, E-stop category and shift. Then they close the energy and thermal budgets with margin, and model the robot's own BMS. The card ends with the power PDR.

This card fixed the five Critical items of the review:
- no soft stop could hold the robot on a slope (R-01);
- a missing LiDAR return counted as clear (R-02);
- the protective field stopped short of the implement, and the blades restarted inside a person hold (R-03);
- the hot-weather results rested on a cooling the sealed pack does not have (R-04);
- one IMU fault disabled the tilt protections (R-05).

The MCU, drive, sensors and GNC engineers fixed them in one pass, recorded as D71 to D73, with 32 new tests and 0 new test failures. The system runs and the independent review confirmed the fixes. The report is NASL-SHD-ER-004.

The vehicle model needs validation where results depend on it:
- the turning coefficient and the turning model (R-18);
- the slope-turn slide check, which cannot fail (R-54);
- the normal loads of the simplified model (R-55);
- the soil-shear delay (R-84).

The descent work added tip-over margins per implement; the tiller still needs real data before any use. The structure engineer closes these, with a turning test on the robot in the field campaign. The card ends with the structure PDR.

The S4 greenhouse mission stopped before its end, for several reasons:
- the implement-ready pause;
- NAV_DEGRADED pauses all through the rows;
- the rejoin replanner finding no path;
- the MCU's yaw loop turning the robot while it was paused.

The MCU, GNC and sensors engineers fixed them (D66 to D69), and the full S4 run completed. The report is NASL-SHD-ER-001.

The digital twin was built in seventeen tasks:
- the integration infrastructure, the world and terrain, and the structure plant;
- the power system and the BMS firmware, the traction drive and the RC link;
- the MCU firmware platform and its vehicle control, the electronics plant and the CAN networks;
- the sensor models, navigation fusion and perception;
- GNC with its mission manager;
- the system integration with scenarios S0 to S8, and the hardware build set-up.

It reached its acceptance on 2026-10-23.

Formal tests on the bench and in the field need approved procedures. The integration engineer writes them with the structure, MCU and comms engineers:
- the row test and the live drive;
- the processor-in-the-loop test;
- a brake test on a slope, which calibrates the tip-over margins of the descent cap.

Each procedure has pass/fail criteria and safety measures, and the owner approves it.

The functional safety engineer turns the safety goals of G1-07 into the functional safety concept. For each safety function (the E-stop chain, STO, the spring brakes, the person stop, the tilt stop, the descent cap, the lost-link stop, the watchdog) it states:
- the safe state and the time to reach it;
- the performance level, and the architecture that gives it: the channels, the diagnostics, the diagnostic coverage;
- its allocation to the hardware, the MCU firmware and the companion;
- how it is verified.

The safety requirements go into the requirements register, traced to the goals. The CDRs of the safety-relevant subsystems design to them.

The model is the main verification tool, but 65 parameter values are marked [EST] in params/, and the audit of the robot against the model comes only at G7 (P-01). The lead systems engineer, with each subsystem engineer for its domain:
- lists every estimated value in a register: its owner, its basis, its uncertainty, and the test or data that will replace it;
- ranks them by the sensitivity of the outputs that safety and the goals rest on: the stop distance and the tip margin on a slope, the person-stop distance, the energy per shift, the tracking error;
- states, for each use of the model in the verification matrix, the credibility it needs and the validation evidence that gives it, in the manner of NASA-STD-7009A;
- specifies the identification tests on the bench and in the field (the slope brake test, the pitch settle, the track-soil parameters, the motor efficiency), which go into the test procedures (T-04).

The functional safety engineer analyses the designs of the safety functions in the Safety Analysis Manager:
- an FMEA of every element of each safety function (the sensors, the MCU logic, the CAN links, the STO of the ESCs, the spring brakes, the E-stop relay, the power rails): each failure mode with its effect, its detection and the diagnostic in the design;
- a fault tree for each top hazard (a person struck, a tip-over on a slope, a runaway downhill), with the single points of failure shown;
- the diagnostic coverage achieved, against the performance level of the concept.

Every gap becomes a card or a change request. The failure modes the design detects are the cases of the fault-injection campaign (T-06).
